WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.190
Narrator: These Lucchese gator boots

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.200
have been worn for about 10 years.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.850
The friction of wear has created a hole

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.290
in the toe area.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.210
They will be restored by Unsung House,

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.620
a Tennessee shoe-repair shop

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.620
that specializes in
restoring cowboy boots.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.893
A cobbler removes the
heel block and outsole

00:00:27.893 --> 00:00:29.070
using nipper pliers

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.179
and a round-point leatherworking knife.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.500
Then he cuts off the boot's gemming,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.150
since it will be replaced later.

00:00:40.246 --> 00:00:42.210
He patches the hole in the boot's toe area

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:45.660
using a special glue
and a piece of leather,

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.252
which he sands to comfortably
blend into the shoe.

00:00:49.818 --> 00:00:51.710
A new gemming is glued on.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.964
Then he sews it in for added security.

00:00:55.964 --> 00:00:58.470
A new leather welt goes over the gemming.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:00:59.790
This creates a layer to attach

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.330
the uppers to the outsole.

00:01:02.715 --> 00:01:05.452
The cobbler then cuts, glues,

00:01:05.452 --> 00:01:08.026
and hammers in new cork midsoles.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.485
The cork is grated,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.631
glued,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.740
and attached to the new outsole.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.570
He uses two machines
to finish the outsole.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.250
One cuts the excess sole,

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.500
and another sews a Goodyear welt.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.240
Then the soles are sanded and dyed.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.030
To finish the restoration,

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.664
he attaches the heel
block with glue and nails.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.050
Once the heels are dyed

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:44.460
to match the rest of the sole,

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.152
the boots are given a final polish.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.340
After a year of heavy wear,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.320
these Birkenstock soles have worn out,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.880
exposing the cork footbed

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.680
and causing serious damage.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.605
To repair them, Rio Kim starts

00:02:03.605 --> 00:02:06.720
by removing the foam outsole with a knife.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.000
He cuts, rather than
peels, the soles apart

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.403
to avoid damaging the
cork footbed further.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.892
He applies glue in the crevice

00:02:13.892 --> 00:02:15.210
of the cracked cork

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.890
and squeezes the joints together

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.083
for one minute, while the glue dries.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.500
For the shoe with the damaged heel,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.320
Rio trims cork footboard to fit the area,

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:26.645
then secures it with glue.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.790
He shapes the cork with a sander

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.505
and 40-grit sandpaper.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.040
Then he uses the old outsole

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.140
to create a pattern for the new ones

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.499
and roughly cuts them out.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.840
Rio sands the foam outsoles

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.310
and bottoms of the shoes

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.140
to even out the surface.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.760
This creates a clean canvas

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.860
and helps the glue stick.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.690
He applies glue to the shoe bottom

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.440
and the foam outsole,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.190
then bends the outsole

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.998
so it fits the curved footbed comfortably.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.400
The body of the shoes and outsole

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.480
are then pressed together,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.346
and Rio cuts the excess off.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:19.170
The last step is sanding the sole to shape

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:22.003
and applying sealant to the cork footbeds.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.370
These well-loved LLBean boots

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.560
are now torn between the sole and upper.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.690
They were mailed back
to the LLBean factory

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.640
for resoling.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.860
A special machine deconstructs the boots

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.935
by sawing the outsoles
apart from the uppers.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.210
A technician then strips the boots

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:55.680
of the remaining brush guard

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.873
using scissors and a flat knife.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:00.660
The remaining stitches

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.900
will be individually plucked
off the uppers by hand.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.180
A rub machine with rotating bristles

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.530
loosens the stitches

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.364
and makes the work easier.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:13.650
With the stitches removed,

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.600
the boots can be reassembled.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.880
Cement is applied to the boot's new sole

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.983
and the upper,

00:04:19.983 --> 00:04:22.233
then the soles and uppers are attached.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.050
To finish, a vamping machine

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:26.850
drops three rows of stitches

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:29.141
to secure the uppers to the sole.

00:04:44.613 --> 00:04:47.310
After these Louboutin loafers
were worn in the rain,

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.900
the paint at the bottom bubbled.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.850
To restore the trademark red bottoms,

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.730
Sunny applies a rubber protective sole.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:54.930
He starts by deciding

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:56.400
where the new protective soles

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.500
and original soles will meet.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.330
He tapes a guide, then uses a knife

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.220
to skive an end joint.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:04.800
This is where the new
thicker protective sole

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.630
will blend into the original.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.070
He follows the same process

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:09.870
on the shoe's heels.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.300
Then he sands the soles and heels

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.475
to remove their paint
and even out the surface.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.220
Sunny applies glue to
the bottom of the shoe

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:21.753
and the new protective soles.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.400
The soles and shoe body

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:27.700
are then pressed together.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:30.960
He trims the excess sole,

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:33.060
then sands it to the shape of the shoe.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.180
To finish, he dresses the edges

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.572
and polishes the shoe.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:00.690
These Air Jordan 11s are from 1996

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:02.490
and have yellowed with age.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.020
To repair the discoloration,

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.780
Richard Brown deconstructs the shoe.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.772
He peels the soles apart
from one another by hand.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:14.820
When the outsoles are removed,

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:17.420
he separates the midsole
from the upper of the shoe.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.132
He deoxidizes the outsoles
using ultraviolet light

00:06:22.132 --> 00:06:25.350
and 40-volume creme, a gel lightener.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.251
This repairs the soles' yellowing.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.320
Then he removes old glue from the outsole,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.930
midsole, and bottom of the shoe

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:36.305
using a rotary tool and acetone.

00:06:41.169 --> 00:06:43.158
The outsoles, midsoles, and uppers

00:06:43.158 --> 00:06:45.210
are reattached using a special glue

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.377
formulated for sneaker repair.

